### Why is blocking IO on commonPool dangerous?

ForkJoinPool.commonPool() has only cores - 1 threads. These threads are shared across the entire JVM. When blocking IO consumes all threads, nothing else can run. Every CompletableFuture, every parallel stream, and every framework that uses commonPool is stalled.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class CommonPoolDangerDemo {

    // Component A: 3 slow JDBC queries on commonPool
    public void componentA() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
                return jdbcQuery("SELECT * FROM settlements WHERE status = 'PENDING'");
                // blocks 2 seconds!
            });
        }
        // All 3 commonPool threads blocked for 2 seconds!
    }

    // Component B: parallel stream (UNRELATED, also uses commonPool)
    public void componentB() {
        var statements = accounts.parallelStream()
            .map(this::generateStatement)  // No threads available!
            .collect(Collectors.toList());
        // STALLED until Component A's JDBC calls finish
    }

    // Component C: CF notification (UNRELATED)
    public void componentC() {
        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> sendNotification());
        // QUEUED behind Component A's blocking calls!
    }
}
```

### Who shares commonPool?

| User | How it uses commonPool |
|------|------------------------|
| `CompletableFuture.supplyAsync()` (no executor) | Default executor |
| `parallelStream()` | Fork-join tasks |
| `Arrays.parallelSort()` | Parallel merge-sort |
| Spring WebFlux internals | Some async callbacks |
| Third-party libraries | Often undocumented |

All compete for the same few threads. A single blocking component can silently degrade the entire application.

### How do you fix it with dedicated pools?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class DedicatedPoolDemo {

    // Dedicated pools per concern
    private static final ExecutorService jdbcPool = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        20, 20, 60L, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(500),
        r -> { Thread t = new Thread(r, "jdbc-pool-" + t.getId()); return t; },
        new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy()
    );

    private static final ExecutorService notifyPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10,
        r -> { Thread t = new Thread(r, "notify-pool"); return t; });

    public void isolatedCalls() {
        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> jdbcQuery(), jdbcPool);     // on jdbcPool
        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> sendNotif(), notifyPool);   // on notifyPool
        accounts.parallelStream().map(this::generateStatement);         // commonPool free!
    }
}
```

### What is the Spring Boot approach?

```java
@Bean("jdbcExecutor")
public Executor jdbcExecutor() {
    ThreadPoolTaskExecutor e = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
    e.setCorePoolSize(20);
    e.setMaxPoolSize(50);
    e.setQueueCapacity(1000);
    e.setThreadNamePrefix("jdbc-");
    e.setRejectionPolicy(new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy());
    return e;
}

@Async("jdbcExecutor")
public CompletableFuture<SettlementData> fetchSettlementData(String tradeId) {
    return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(settlementRepo.findByTradeId(tradeId));
}
```

### Can you increase commonPool's size?

Yes, via the system property `java.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool.common.parallelism`. But this is a workaround, not a fix. Increasing the pool size just delays the problem because blocking tasks can always fill any pool. Dedicated pools with proper isolation is the correct approach.

### What is the rule?

`supplyAsync(task)` with no pool = commonPool = dangerous for blocking IO. `supplyAsync(task, ioPool)` = dedicated pool = safe. Every blocking call (JDBC, HTTP, gRPC, file IO) must specify a dedicated executor.

### What are edge cases?

Work-stealing does not help with blocking. ForkJoinPool's work-stealing assumes tasks complete quickly and return to the deque. A blocked thread cannot steal from others and cannot be stolen from. The pool effectively loses that worker for the duration of the blocking call.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can parallelStream use a custom pool? Yes, submit it inside a custom ForkJoinPool: `customPool.submit(() -> stream.parallel()...)`. The stream operations run on that custom pool's workers.
- How do virtual threads change this? Virtual threads make blocking cheap because the JVM unmounts a blocked virtual thread from its carrier. But until virtual threads are widely adopted, dedicated platform-thread pools remain the standard approach.
